#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175691 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5517548-8939-5bf5-a769-9f99a2aa1029" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where the Tangent Is Vertical"}] :question/id #uuid "f67abf5c-1352-5fd0-8365-0164c47a28a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10245" :question/problem "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi]$ for which the polar curve $r=\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta$ has vertical tangents, given that\n$x'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)-\\sin(2\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)+\\sin(2\\theta)$." :question/worked-solution "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad y'(\\theta) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta)$.\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)-\\sin(2\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta)+\\sin(2\\theta)\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=0\n$$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (2θ) - \\sin (2θ) &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin (2θ) &= \\cos (2θ) \\\\\n\\tan (2θ) &= 1 \\\\\n2θ &= \\frac{π}{4} + nπ \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{π}{8} + \\frac{nπ}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only two belong to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8},\\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into $y'(\\theta)$ and check that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nfurther investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 =\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$, we get\n$y^{′}(\\frac{π}{8})|= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{4}); = \\sqrt{2}; \\ne 0.✓$\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 =\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ is a valid solution.\n- Substituting $\\theta_2 =\\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}$, we get\n$y^{′}(\\frac{5π}{8})|= \\cos (\\frac{5π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{5π}{4}); =-\\sqrt{2}; \\ne 0.✓$\nTherefore, $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}$ is also a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our curve has vertical tangents at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\n![](images/53/53562c50591938dcedf979a1dd4f0c1a9f0e43cade71e36f3f908bb0595a8794.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175793 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca410330-0448-5503-b5f6-37021667a130" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where the Tangent Is Horizontal"}] :question/id #uuid "ba03d3ad-adb7-5c30-9d44-181a1980d125" :question/math-academy-id "e-630" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f8a8203c3684107378ff709e3c2675a767cbbfe03fc93b3665abcaa7ac13054.png)\n\nCompute the value of $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi]$ for which the polar curve $r=e^{-\\theta}$ (shown above) has a horizontal tangent, given that\n$x'(\\theta)=-e^{-\\theta}(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta)=e^{-\\theta}(-\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta)$." :question/worked-solution "We have a horizontal tangent when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta)$.\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-e^{-\\theta}(\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta)=e^{-\\theta}(-\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta)\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta)=0\n$$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{- θ}(-\\sin θ + \\cos θ) &= 0 \\\\\n- \\sin θ + \\cos θ &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin θ &= \\cos θ \\\\\n\\tan θ &= 1 \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{π}{4} + πn\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into $x'(\\theta)$ and check that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nfurther investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, we get\n$x^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})|=-e^{- π/4}(\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{4})); =-e^{- π/4}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}); =-\\sqrt{2}e^{- π/4}; \\ne 0.✓$\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our curve has a horizontal tangent at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n![](images/db/db7dea31d2eaeff23f4ebad30e2ea4deff098886a3a38b9d353af64307144939.png)"}]}
